WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:02.840
Professor Hodgkin...

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.200
...should be much better known
than she is.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.600
Scientists admire the great
determination and skill

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.480
which has always been
the mark of your work.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.200
She was a highly intelligent,
highly focused scientist.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.200
She would keep going,
whatever the difficulties.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.040
You just solve the next small problem

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.320
and eventually the whole problem
will crack.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.320
Involving what can only be described
as gifted intuition.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.000
She was a peace lover.
She believed in solving problems

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.800
by dialogue and not by confrontation.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.720
In recognition of your
services to science...

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.880
She's the only British woman
scientist ever to have won

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.240
a Nobel prize...

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.360
...For chemistry.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.560
What was it about Dorothy
that made is possible

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.760
for her to achieve

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:49.720
the things she did,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.920
at a time when so few women
had those opportunities?

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.680
Dorothy was born in Cairo,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.840
her father was very interested
in archaeology

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.400
as was her mother.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.600
If there was a dig, archaeological
dig, she would try and join in.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.800
She also had this amazing ability

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.920
for recognising patterns
and symmetry,

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.800
and her notebooks show that, from
when she was really quite young.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.360
When World War One broke out,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.200
Dorothy and her then two sisters

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.800
were brought back to England.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.160
She was essentially left

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.440
as the head of the family.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.400
She had to worry about whether there
was enough money in the bank account.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.040
Her interest in chemistry

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.200
started when she was only
about the age of 10,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.040
when she went to a little
tiny primary school

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.120
where they grew crystals

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.560
and she said herself,
"I was captured for life

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.640
by chemistry and by crystals."

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.120
When Dorothy was in her teens,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.720
one of the discoverers
of x-ray crystallography

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.560
talked about this technique

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.560
that allows you to see where the
atoms are in the molecule

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.120
and how they're arranged in space,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.560
and so she read about
being able to see atoms

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.480
and said to herself there and then,
"That's what I want to do."

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.720
In lectures she was
extremely well-known

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.160
for seemingly having gone to sleep,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.160
and yet at the end, Dorothy would ask
the most piercing question.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.880
She clearly wasn't asleep.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.000
What personal qualities have
helped you in the work?

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.400
In some ways, I suppose, a certain
kind of foolhardiness

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.960
for going on, doing things

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.760
that other people don't expect
is quite possible to do.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.240
I think a lot of girls grow up
with a sense

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.040
that they don't have the permission
to do things they might want to do.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.400
One of the characteristics that
Dorothy's upbringing gave her

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.040
was a tremendous sense of agency.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.240
When she got married,

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.320
she was asked to stand down
from her fellowship at college.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.920
Eventually this was changed,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.560
and she also managed to be awarded
maternity leave.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.920
She was the first woman to have
that at the University of Oxford.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.920
It paved the way for other women

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.760
who wanted to have a fulfilled
scientific life

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.120
and also to have a family.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.240
After Dorothy's first child was born,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.960
she suffered an attack of acute
rheumatoid arthritis

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.640
and left her with distorted
hands and feet.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.880
As she got older,
the arthritis did recur

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.800
but she didn't let it hold her back.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.040
Dorothy was very much engaged
in international issues

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.960
and so she was vehemently opposed
to the war in Vietnam

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.760
and indeed visited north Vietnam.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.120
She did travel extensively and
she made a point of visiting,

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.680
first of all, the Soviet Union,
and subsequently China.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.120
It was the height of the Cold War,
but scientific relations continued

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.480
and she was always very keen
to make contact.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.600
Internationalism was a very big part
of her make-up.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.520
Dorothy remains the only
woman scientist

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.360
in this country to win a Nobel prize.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.600
The determination of the
structure of vitamin B12

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.240
has been considered
the crowning triumph

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.600
of x-ray crystallographic analysis.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.400
The Daily Mail ran the headline

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.880
'Housewife wins Nobel prize'.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.480
The reaction of newspapers in the 60s

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.880
whenever women achieved anything

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:22.200
was absolutely appalling.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.200
Dorothy's influence
on modern medicine

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.920
is almost incalculable.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.160
All the problems that Dorothy
chose to work on

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.680
were problems that would contribute

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.000
to a better understanding
of the body in health and disease.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.960
Penicillin. The knowledge of its
structure was enormously important

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:43.440
in the Second World War.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.240
Its structure was not understood
until she solved it.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.800
It enabled doctors to use materials
that had been synthesised

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.000
in a laboratory and apply those
to the sick patients.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.760
The way in which those drugs
are made now

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.640
rely a lot on the structure that
Dorothy determined.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.560
She gave the impression

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.320
to those who didn't know her perhaps

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.960
of being a frail old lady,
which of course she wasn't.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:14.880
There was nothing frail

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.280
about Dorothy's mind, attitude,
kindness and so on.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.880
Dorothy should be remembered
for blazing a trail, really,

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.320
for showing that women
can be scientists

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.040
and not only be scientists, but be
extremely successful scientists.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.360
She gave to the world
the knowledge

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.400
but also the way to do it,

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.480
the determination not to give up.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.640
If you know and think you can do it,
keep working at it. 

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.800
And if that's not the definition
of exceptional,

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.480
I'm not sure what is.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.000
Thank you, Doctor Hodgkin.

